module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_request_rewrite_that_reencodes_the_body_it_edits_reorders_the_keys_downstream_reads

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

// A PROXY THAT EDITS ONE FIELD OF A JSON REQUEST BODY BY DECODING IT, MUTATING A TABLE AND RE-ENCODING IT
// REPLACES EVERY BYTE OF THAT BODY, AND NOTHING AT THE REQUEST BOUNDARY SAYS SO. Filed 2026-10-31 by
// proud-bear-756 at the manager's direction (calm-crab-645, msg_b18efa42), after the defect was found in
// the srv1 dev router and fixed by calm-moth-166 in the same stack (PR #15113, merged as 6975bc9e610e).
// This row files the CLASS and its executed RED; the fix is not this row's work and is not claimed here.

data a_request_rewrite_that_reencodes_the_body_it_edits_reorders_the_keys_downstream_reads: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_request_rewrite_that_reencodes_the_body_it_edits_reorders_the_keys_downstream_reads" as NonEmptyStr,

  // FOUND SILENTLY WRONG, SO BELOW THE LADDER. No status code, no log line, no diagnostic marked the
  // state: the edited request parsed, routed, and answered 200. What moved was a number nobody reads --
  // the upstream engine's cached-token count -- and it moved in a way that looked like an engine defect.
  // The ceiling is MechanicallyPreventable rather than aspirational: one executed case pair refuses the
  // state, and it did (see the control receipt below).
  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder,
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundBelowTheLadder,
      ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "the capability, already exercised once by hand, of running the router proxy falsifier's claim rows on the required path so that a forwarded-body byte-fidelity pair -- one request whose model id enters the rewrite and one control that does not, each asserting the upstream received the client's own bytes with only the named field replaced -- refuses any checkout that reintroduces a whole-body re-encode, while remaining INDIFFERENT to whether the rewrite itself is ever wanted"
    },
  },

  receipts: [
    "**INVALID STATE.** A routing policy that must change one field of a JSON body does it as decode, mutate,
    re-encode: at 50043a419018734c846f483c06fb97cc68335fa0 the access-phase lines ended the alias rewrite
    with 'if changed then ngx.req.set_body_data(cjson.encode(body)) end' (dag/gunbc/serving/router_dev_policy.dag,
    pre-fix line 267, read in that blob). The bytes that reach the upstream are not the client's bytes with
    one field changed; they are a fresh serialization of a Lua table. Object key ORDER is an artifact of the
    encoder's traversal, and so are whitespace and number formatting, so a rewrite aimed at one key silently
    rewrites every other byte. The request still parses and still answers, so the boundary that could have
    spoken has nothing to say.",

    "**THE HARM, AND WHY IT POINTED SOMEWHERE ELSE.** Upstream engines build a prompt from the request in
    RECEIVED order -- Strata renders 'tools' in the order the keys arrive -- so a key reorder changes the
    prompt text, which changes the prefix the engine's KV cache is keyed by. Reported by calm-moth-166 on
    2026-10-30: h11 agent sessions reused 0 cached tokens on roughly half of their turns, which read as an
    engine or cache defect. The turns that lost reuse were the ones whose model id was the router's alias
    and so entered the rewrite; the turns sent with a direct model id kept their reuse. The router was the
    only thing between the two populations and nobody had looked at the router's BYTES.",

    "**WHAT MADE IT LOOK SAFE.** The instrument had byte-fidelity cases, and they held. Both were sent with a
    DIRECT model id, so the rewrite branch never ran; and each assertion is a 'contains' over the forwarded
    body, which a re-encoding satisfies as readily as a splice. A green case set that checks for the
    presence of substrings says nothing about the bytes in between -- see the companion row
    'a_green_case_set_that_counts_row_identities_and_never_the_branches_those_rows_take' for that class.",

    "**THE FIX AS LANDED (calm-moth-166, not this row's claim).** An edit is a SPLICE on the raw bytes:
    'forwarded(pick)' in router_dev_access_lines now finds the one occurrence of the named key and rebuilds
    the string from the untouched prefix and suffix, and the alias and effort rewrites both go through it.
    The plan already said the rule was 'Body bytes remain unchanged' (gunbc.plans.fabric_serving_router);
    the fix made the code match the plan rather than the other way round.",

    "**THE DISCRIMINATING RED, EXECUTED.** The pair now in falsifier_cases:
    'dev_forwarded_body_is_the_client_bytes_when_nothing_is_rewritten' (control: direct model id, rewrite
    not entered, upstream reports the body it received) and 'dev_alias_and_effort_rewrites_splice_the_client_bytes'
    (aliased model id plus a reasoning_effort inside chat_template_kwargs: the upstream must report the
    client's body with exactly the named field spliced in, key order included). calm-moth-166 ran the
    control on srv1: same tree, one file restored to the pre-fix revision, and EXACTLY the four new cases
    fail with everything else holding. That is what makes this RED discriminating rather than decorative --
    a reintroduced re-encode moves these cases, and only these.",

    "**CEILING AND TRIGGER.** The ceiling is MechanicallyPreventable and nothing higher is available at the
    current substrate: the proxy model carries Lua as 'LuaPhaseBlock { phase: LuaPhase, lines: List<String> }'
    (extdeps.proxy.openresty), so no type makes a whole-body re-encode unrepresentable while policy is
    authored as lines. The blocker is ClimbableButUnbuilt, not AwaitsOneGrounding: the executing mechanism
    exists and has run by hand; what is missing is the required-path step that runs it, since CI's
    v2-bootstrap executes no claim rows. The second, higher-value step is the modeled redesign's: make the
    rewrite unnecessary by having engines accept the alias, which removes the body edit instead of guarding
    it -- that is calm-moth-166's plan, and this row does not claim credit for it.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.serving.router_dev_policy", decl_name: "router_dev_access_lines"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.instruments.router_proxy_falsifier", decl_name: "falsifier_cases"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "gunbc.plans.fabric_serving_router", decl_name: "fabric_serving_router_plan"),
    decl_ref(module_path: "extdeps.proxy.openresty", decl_name: "LuaPhaseBlock"),
  ],
}
